  "worked_solution": "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-1{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|2-1{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|1{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[1,2].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{15}{8}{x}^{-7/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{15}{8{x}^{7/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[1,2],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(1)|=\\frac{15}{8(1)^{7/2}}=\\frac{15}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{15}{8})}{6}|1{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.313\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nup to three decimal places.",
